<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD polkit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/software/polkit/policyconfig-1.dtd">

<!--
  The polkit action behind the enrollment application.

  Without this file pkexec still works, but it asks "Authentication is
  required to run /usr/libexec/ubuntuguard/ubuntuguard-enroll-root as the
  super user", which tells an employee nothing about what they are approving.
  A password prompt whose text the person cannot evaluate is a prompt they
  learn to click through, so the message matters.

  auth_admin rather than auth_admin_keep: the approval is not cached, so a
  second enrollment attempt asks again. The operation runs once per laptop,
  so there is no repetition to spare the employee, and avoiding an unprompted
  window in which a root helper can be re-invoked is worth more than the one
  saved password entry.

  allow_inactive is "no" so the action cannot be taken from an inactive
  session: a different user switched away from, or a lingering session on
  another VT. Enrollment is something the person at the keyboard does.

  The action is safe to expose at all because the helper takes no arguments.
  Its only input is the enrollment token on stdin, so there is nothing an
  attacker who can invoke it could ask it to do; the worst available outcome
  is consuming a token they already hold.
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>UbuntuGuard</vendor>

  <action id="com.ubuntuguard.enroll">
    <description>Enrol this laptop with UbuntuGuard</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to enrol this laptop with UbuntuGuard and start its device-management services.</message>
    <icon_name>security-high</icon_name>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/libexec/ubuntuguard/ubuntuguard-enroll-root</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
